---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('attentive')!;
const b = appBySlug('sendlane')!;
const path = '/compare/attentive-vs-sendlane';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/attentive-vs-sendlane');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/attentive-vs-sendlane');
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Attentive or Sendlane for a new Shopify program?', answer: 'Evaluate Attentive for enabled email and SMS journeys. Privy has acquired Sendlane; ask which platform, contract and onboarding path a new customer would receive before treating historical Sendlane features as the offered product.' },
  { question: 'Does Attentive support email recovery?', answer: 'Yes. Attentive documents email abandonment journeys and combined email/SMS checkout journeys. Its email-step documentation asks merchants to contact the team for access, so confirm enablement in the account being quoted.' },
  { question: 'Should an existing Sendlane customer migrate immediately?', answer: 'The acquisition alone does not establish a migration deadline or feature loss. Confirm account-specific continuity, support and export arrangements with the provider before changing live automations.' },
  { question: 'What timing and frequency cap should we use?', answer: 'Choose waits from customer context, measured event delay and applicable channel restrictions. Review all competing campaigns and pending messages; the examples here do not establish a universal schedule or cap.' },
  { question: 'How does the discount model work?', answer: 'For a hypothetical $623 basket at 60% product margin, unchanged product cost is $249.20. Full-price product gross profit is $373.80; a 16% discount removes $99.68 and leaves $274.12 before shipping, fees, returns and platform costs.' },
  { question: 'Have these workflows been tested?', answer: 'No. They are proposed acceptance exercises for a demonstration or controlled pilot. Record event receipt, eligibility, queued actions and purchase exits rather than presenting these scenarios as measured recovery results.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Compare an Attentive email/SMS journey with the actual Sendlane or Privy account offered to your store. For existing Sendlane merchants, continuity and the cost of moving a working program matter as much as a feature list.</p>
    <div class="mt-10"><TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'Channel evaluation', tool: a.name, reason: 'Confirm email access, checkout events and coordinated SMS exits in the quoted account.' }, { label: 'Account continuity', tool: b.name, reason: 'Clarify the Privy acquisition, new-account destination and existing-account support before choosing a migration.' }]} /></div>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Attentive</th><th>Sendlane / Privy</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Published scope</td><td>Email and SMS abandonment journeys</td><td>Sendlane markets email, SMS and forms; Privy has acquired the business</td></tr>
        <tr><td>New account</td><td>Confirm selected channels and email enablement</td><td>Confirm receiving platform, available journeys and contract</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Existing program</td><td>Rebuild and rehearse any incoming journey</td><td>Confirm continuity and support before moving live flows</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Budget evidence</td><td>Quote matched to list, sends, channels and AI options</td><td>Current written offer for the actual account and migration scope</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <h2>Resolve the account destination first</h2>
      <p>Privy’s <a href="https://www.privy.com/sendlane-acquisition">acquisition announcement</a> confirms that it acquired Sendlane. The <a href="https://www.sendlane.com/">Sendlane site</a> still describes email, SMS, forms and cart automation. Those pages do not establish the exact product a new merchant will receive, or an existing merchant’s migration date. Ask sales to name the platform, demonstrate its Shopify checkout trigger and attach the available features and support scope to the offer. Checked November 25, 2026.</p>
      <p>An existing Sendlane customer should inventory live journeys, forms, sender identities, suppression lists, pending messages and exports before evaluating a move. Ask who supports those flows during any transition and whether identifiers and historical events survive. Do not replace a functioning program solely because an acquisition occurred; compare the demonstrated destination with the operational cost of staying.</p>
      <h2>Attentive email is part of the comparison</h2>
      <p>Attentive documents <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4410320387732-Email-abandonment-journeys">email abandonment and combined email/SMS checkout journeys</a>. Its <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4408727834772-Emails-in-journeys">email-step guide</a> instructs merchants to contact the team for access. Confirm enablement before planning the flow. A phone-number match is not evidence of email permission, and an email subscription does not establish SMS eligibility.</p>
      <h2>Use a checkout trace as the demonstration</h2>
      <p>These are proposed exercises, not tests we performed. Use controlled profiles for an email-only subscriber, a dual-channel subscriber and a suppressed customer. Abandon a checkout and record the cart/customer key, event time, arrival time, eligible channel and queued action. Then purchase while the reminder waits. Require the operator to show what stops, where the purchase was received and how a repeated checkout event is deduplicated.</p>
      <p>For an existing Sendlane store considering Attentive, replay the same cases against the offered destination. Carry permission provenance and suppression evidence across the handoff. Disable the old journey before releasing its replacement; reconcile pending sends explicitly and give one operator rollback ownership. An arbitrary two-week overlap is not an acceptance criterion.</p>
      <h2>Keep the useful checkout objections</h2>
      <p>For a bundle discount missing from a $623 cart, send the cart contents and expected offer to the support owner. Resolve eligibility and stacking before proposing another code. For a financing widget abandoned at review, use approved financing information and a stable checkout link; do not imply approval or invent urgency. A B2B basket below minimum order quantity needs a purchasing or sales handoff rather than repeated consumer reminders.</p>
      <p>For international shipping shock, have support verify the destination quote. For a subscription toggle left on, ask the customer to confirm the intended cadence rather than silently changing terms. A size question needs sizing guidance; an unavailable variant needs an availability decision. If every item is unavailable, stop the original offer. A gift-message failure needs a working input path, and a repeat buyer who already ordered needs a purchase exit. These cases preserve the reason for abandonment instead of assigning a different channel to each scenario.</p>
      <h2>Coordinate channels around pending work</h2>
      <p>Assign one owner to email and one to SMS, including Shopify’s own reminders and any other sender. Choose waits after measuring event lag and checking applicable quiet hours and channel restrictions. Attentive’s <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4410869137428-Carrier-guidelines-for-abandonment-messages">carrier guidance</a> documents restrictions on abandonment texts; a generic three-touch ladder is not a substitute. Test an opt-out and a purchase while actions are queued. Adding push requires its own verified subscription and queue ownership, not an assumed escalation step.</p>
      <h2>Separate product margin from recovery lift</h2>
      <p>Hypothetical product gross profit equals basket value × (product margin rate − discount rate), assuming unchanged product cost. A $623 basket at 60% margin has $249.20 product cost and $373.80 full-price gross profit. A 16% discount costs $99.68 and leaves $274.12. A second $485 basket at 54% margin has $223.10 product cost and $261.90 full-price gross profit; a hypothetical 9% discount costs $43.65 and leaves $218.25. Neither discount is a recommended cap.</p>
      <p>The model excludes shipping, taxes, payment fees, refunds, returns, staff and platform costs. Keep those costs separate in the pilot ledger. Reconcile order IDs and competing attribution windows; a platform-credited order may have returned without the reminder. Size a randomized unmessaged comparison for the decision you need, rather than imposing a fixed 5% holdout. Compare incremental contribution and uncertainty alongside operator effort.</p>
      <h2>Request comparable commercial offers</h2>
      <p><a href="https://www.attentive.com/pricing">Attentive pricing</a> varies with messages, subscriber count, selected channels and AI products. Request the same expected audience and send volume from both providers, identifying onboarding, migration, support, overages and any separate channel charges. Use the actual Sendlane/Privy offer instead of an undated starting price. Choose after the account destination, permission handoff, purchase exit and total operating cost have been demonstrated.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Attentive vs Sendlane FAQ" />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
